<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William II of Villehardouin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1278">1278</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William II of Villehardouin was Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> in the Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> from 1246 to 1278. He completed the conquest of the Peloponnese with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> in 1248 and built castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mystras">Mistras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-magne">Grand Magne</a>. Captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelagonia">Battle of Pelagonia</a> in 1259, he gained his release by ceding Mistras, Monemvasia and Grand Magne, the base of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/despotate-of-the-morea">Despotate of the Morea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-villehardouin.jpg" alt="William II of Villehardouin" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_II_of_Achaea_coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Geoffrey’s younger son <b>William II of Villehardouin</b> completed the conquest by taking Monemvasia in 1248 after a long siege, and built the fortresses of Mistras, Grand Magne and Beaufort to control the Mani and the Slavic Melingoi of the Taygetus range.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He was the model knight of the Chronicle of the Morea, admired for his courage despite rebelling against Prince <b>William II</b> and eloping with a vassal's wife.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-karytaina">Barony of Karytaina</a></li><li><blockquote>Its lords ruled from the castle over Greek villages, alongside the castle of Grand Magne built by <b>William II of Villehardouin</b> further south.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-passavant">Barony of Passavant</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Frankish conquest, <b>William of Villehardouin</b> built Mistras and other castles in 1249 partly to control them; they later served as soldiers for both Franks and Byzantines.</blockquote><a href="/state/melingoi">Melingoi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalamata">Kalamata</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalamata">Kalamata</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.04,22.11&amp;year=1268" aria-label="Kalamata on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5104 830 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William II of Villehardouin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5554 1100h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5554 1100h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.04,22.11&amp;year=1268">What was here in 1268? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
